<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 2.3 - The Span of a Set of Vectors</title>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <em>span</em> of a set of vectors
                <m>\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \ldots, \vvec_n</m> is the set of all
                linear combinations that can be formed from the vectors.
            </p>

            <p>
                Alternatively, if
                <m>{A = \left[\vvec_1 \,\,\, \vvec_2 \,\,\, \cdots \,\,\, \vvec_n\right],}</m>
                then the span of the vectors consists of all vectors
                <m>\bvec</m> for which the equation <m>A\xvec = \bvec</m> is
                consistent.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's put some more technical terms to the magic
                carpet/hoverboard scenario.
                Recall the following movement:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            You and your cousin's hoverboard travel in the
                            direction <m>\vvec = \twovec{3}{1}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Your magic carpet can move in the direction
                            <m>\wvec = \twovec{1}{2}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Your cousin's magic carpet can move in the direction
                            <m>\uvec = \twovec{6}{2}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe the set of vectors that result from linear
                    combinations of <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    All of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe the set of vectors that result from linear
                    combinations of <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\uvec</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    A line, with the slope 1/3, going through the origin.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is Span <m>\{\vvec,\wvec\}</m>?
                    Try to describe geometrically and algebraically.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    Geometrically, this is all of <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
                    Algebraically, this is
                    <m>{a\twovec{3}{1} + b\twovec{1}{2}}</m> for any real number
                    <m>a, b</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is Span <m>\{\vvec,\uvec\}</m>?
                    Try to describe geometrically and algebraically.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    Geometrically, this is a line.
                    Algebraically, this is <m>{a\twovec{3}{1}}</m> for any real
                    number <m>a</m>.
                    Note, that we don't need the <m>\uvec</m> because it is a
                    scalar multiple of <m>\vvec</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For what vectors <m>\bvec</m> does the matrix equation
                    <m>\left[
    \begin{array}{rr}
      3 \amp 1  \\
      1 \amp 2 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]\xvec = \bvec</m>
                    have a solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    All vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For what vectors <m>\bvec</m> does the matrix equation
                    <m>\left[
    \begin{array}{rr}
      3 \amp 6  \\
      1 \amp 2 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]\xvec = \bvec</m>
                    have a solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    All vectors <m>\bvec = \twovec{3x}{x}</m> for any value
                    <m>x</m> (a line).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many pivots does the matrix
                    <m>\left[
    \begin{array}{rr}
      3 \amp 1  \\
      1 \amp 2 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]</m>
                    have?
                    How might this be related to your answer for Span
                    <m>\{\vvec,\wvec\}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The row reduced echelon form of
                    <m>\left[
    \begin{array}{rr}
      3 \amp 1  \\
      1 \amp 2 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]</m>
                    is the identity matrix, which means that the system is
                    always consistent, no matter what <m>\bvec</m> is.
                    There is a pivot in every row!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many pivots does the matrix
                    <m>\left[
    \begin{array}{rr}
      3 \amp 6  \\
      1 \amp 2 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]</m>
                    have?
                    How might this be related to your answer for Span
                    <m>\{\vvec,\uvec\}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="08-solutions">
                <p>
                    The row reduced echelon form of
                    <m>\left[
    \begin{array}{rr}
      3 \amp 1  \\
      1 \amp 2 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]</m>
                    is
                    <m>\left[
    \begin{array}{rr}
      1 \amp 2  \\
      0 \amp 0 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]</m>,
                    which means that there is only 1 pivot and thus, not always
                    a solution.
                    For some <m>\bvec</m> there could be a pivot in the
                    rightmost column of the augmented matrix for the
                    corresponding linear system, thus resulting in no solution.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the span of a set of vectors and how do we denote
                    it?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How can we determine if a vector is in the span of another
                    set of vectors?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we know if the span of a set of vectors
                    <m>\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \dots, \vvec_n</m> is
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m> using the pivots of the matrix
                    <m>[\vvec_1 \,\,\, \vvec_2 \,\,\, \vvec_3 \,\,\, \dots \,\,\, \vvec_n]</m>?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>